    <!--todo move stuff to Celosia.Lang-->
    <!--Note to translators: (todo move this to a separate md file)
        This game's battle mechanics make an effort to use extremely consistent language and formatting across all situations so that someone familiar with the game's terminology can interpret mechanics much more quickly
        Please try to keep this consistency as best you can (and if you see a place where I broke my own rules, please let me know so I can correct it)
        This game makes many references. Most references are notated in this file, to make them much easier to preserve in translation
        Many skills/passives also share names with skills/passives from Pokémon and Shin Megami Tensei, which is not notated in every case
        You don't have to preserve every reference in translation, since some may not make sense when translated
        For skills/etc where the name is not a reference, it is not particularly important that the translation keeps the exact same meaning as long as it conveys the same vibe
        (For instance, "Thunder Spear" could easily be replaced with anything that just as strongly implies a Str-based Fulgur attack; just make it sound cool and intuitive)

    Quick guide to text formatting:
        Only change the content in the <value> tags. Do not change the <data name=""> field
        Text is whitespace-sensitive, so mind indentation/newlines
        /c[white] or /c[#ffffff] is a color
        /i[image-name] is an image
        {0} embeds the 1st parameter, {1} the 2nd, and so on. A description of each parameter is usually written above the entry
        
        Selection formats:
            Some entries use ICU MessageFormat for advanced formatting: https://unicode-org.github.io/icu/userguide/format_parse/messages/
            I only use a very small subset of its features, which are summarized below
            If you don't understand this, it's probably fine; you shouldn't have to edit them. Just change the contents of their inner {} and nothing else
            
            Example selector that checks parameter 0:
                {0,plural,=0{it's exactly zero}=1{it's exactly one}other{it's #}
                # prints the parameter itself, so if you provide 50, you'll get "it's 50"

                {0,select,foo{lorem}bar{ipsum}other{dolor}}
                A basic selector
                
                {0,number} formats a number
                {0,number,integer} formats as int
                {0,number,percent} formats as percentage
                These aren't text, so they won't need editing-->
